{"exhaustive":{"nbHits":false,"typo":false},"exhaustiveNbHits":false,"exhaustiveTypo":false,"hits":[{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"stass"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai","agent","cost"],"value":"For many of the projects I work on I found myself wanting to use a relational language like Datalog to generate C source from specifications, do verification during build or maintain a task database.<p>However pulling a full blown Souffle or SWI-PL as a build dependency is always a PITA and also an overkill.<p>So I (with the help of Claude) built awklog, a pure POSIX awk based interpreter.  Copy it to your project and use during build stage or test stage with no extra dependencies.<p>It's a semi-naive interpreter, so decently fast, and certainly fast enough for anything you might need to do as a dev tool.<p>One cool usecase in particular I've been a heavy user of is a task system maintained directly in the project git repo.  Just create a text file with tuples describing your tasks, their states, dependencies, hypotheses and so on.  AWKLog will be able to query it for you, even recursively, and provide instant answers.  Easy for <em>AI</em> <em>agents</em> to query too and no remote infrastructure dependencies at all.  Everything stays in GIT, version <em>cont</em>rolled.<p>I hope other people will find this tool useful too."},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: AWKLog \u2013 a minimalist Datalog interpreter in Awk"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://github.com/stass/awklog"}},"_tags":["story","author_stass","story_50333096","show_hn"],"author":"stass","created_at":"2026-11-02T04:25:07Z","created_at_i":1793593507,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50333096","points":1,"story_id":50333096,"story_text":"For many of the projects I work on I found myself wanting to use a relational language like Datalog to generate C source from specifications, do verification during build or maintain a task database.<p>However pulling a full blown Souffle or SWI-PL as a build dependency is always a PITA and also an overkill.<p>So I (with the help of Claude) built awklog, a pure POSIX awk based interpreter.  Copy it to your project and use during build stage or test stage with no extra dependencies.<p>It&#x27;s a semi-naive interpreter, so decently fast, and certainly fast enough for anything you might need to do as a dev tool.<p>One cool usecase in particular I&#x27;ve been a heavy user of is a task system maintained directly in the project git repo.  Just create a text file with tuples describing your tasks, their states, dependencies, hypotheses and so on.  AWKLog will be able to query it for you, even recursively, and provide instant answers.  Easy for AI agents to query too and no remote infrastructure dependencies at all.  Everything stays in GIT, version controlled.<p>I hope other people will find this tool useful too.","title":"Show HN: AWKLog \u2013 a minimalist Datalog interpreter in Awk","updated_at":"2026-11-02T04:29:27Z","url":"https://github.com/stass/awklog"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"dataflow"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai","agent","cost"],"value":"&gt; understands it thoroughly, and uses it to develop interesting further questions or directions<p>He phrases it like these criteria are requirements, but they really <em>aren't</em> for every problem. They make sense for those theoretical problems whose solutions' main values lie in their understandability and further development, but not every problem is like that. Some problems' solutions are just revolutionary on their own even if you <em>cont</em>ribute or understand absolutely nothing else. <em>AI</em> technology itself is an example of this!<p>To give a trivial hypothetical counterexample: if you came up with a solution for the traveling salesman problem that is both empirically sufficiently faster than the state of the art, that is an immense <em>cont</em>ribution and you absolutely deserve the credit for it regardless of whether you or anyone else understands how/why it works. It could literally even produce wrong results 50% of the time and still revolutionize humanity if it's efficient enough when it's correct. Denying them credit because they didn't understand the algorithm would be ludicrous - the results stand &amp; speak for themselves.<p>Statisticians, traditional-<em>AI</em> researchers, etc. had to learn this the hard way when (basically black-box) neural nets became so good that their otherwise-elegant &amp; explainable classical algorithms just stopped being able to compete in more and more applications, and got left behind. Are mathematicians looking to repeat that history?"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Mathematics has changed. Let's act like it"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2026/11/01/mathematics-has-changed-lets-act-like-it/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_dataflow","story_50328251"],"author":"dataflow","children":[50332917],"comment_text":"&gt; understands it thoroughly, and uses it to develop interesting further questions or directions<p>He phrases it like these criteria are requirements, but they really aren&#x27;t for every problem. They make sense for those theoretical problems whose solutions&#x27; main values lie in their understandability and further development, but not every problem is like that. Some problems&#x27; solutions are just revolutionary on their own even if you contribute or understand absolutely nothing else. AI technology itself is an example of this!<p>To give a trivial hypothetical counterexample: if you came up with a solution for the traveling salesman problem that is both empirically sufficiently faster than the state of the art, that is an immense contribution and you absolutely deserve the credit for it regardless of whether you or anyone else understands how&#x2F;why it works. It could literally even produce wrong results 50% of the time and still revolutionize humanity if it&#x27;s efficient enough when it&#x27;s correct. Denying them credit because they didn&#x27;t understand the algorithm would be ludicrous - the results stand &amp; speak for themselves.<p>Statisticians, traditional-AI researchers, etc. had to learn this the hard way when (basically black-box) neural nets became so good that their otherwise-elegant &amp; explainable classical algorithms just stopped being able to compete in more and more applications, and got left behind. Are mathematicians looking to repeat that history?","created_at":"2026-11-02T03:07:51Z","created_at_i":1793588871,"objectID":"50332690","parent_id":50328796,"story_id":50328251,"story_title":"Mathematics has changed. Let's act like it","story_url":"https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2026/11/01/mathematics-has-changed-lets-act-like-it/","updated_at":"2026-11-02T04:49:57Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"bitwize"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai","agent","cost"],"value":"...For now.<p>Frontier models are generating sensible abstract engineering decisions for nontrivial code bases. Phase 1 (GPT 4 era) was &quot;fancy autocomplete&quot;: it could generate a function or complete a clause before losing the plot. Phase 2 (Opus 4.x era) was the spec-driven development era, involving careful <em>const</em>ruction of guardrails and guidelines and steering the <em>agents</em> as they worked. Phase 3 is the beginning of the &quot;software factory&quot; era. You can now one-shot nontrivial apps with a prompt that leaves most of the specifics up to the <em>agents</em>. You just can't do this for like, a whole enterprise\u2014maybe a microservice.<p>Another phase or two, and you can pretty much say goodbye to 95% of SWE jobs. The only people left involved in the software development process will be business analysts, product managers, and perhaps designers if they don't want the UI to look all &quot;<em>AI</em> ass-ugly&quot;. A handful of trillion-dollar companies will retain a few really smart people who <i>could</i> code by hand if they wanted to, to keep the models trained and topped up on good practices. But line-of-business, which was the bread and butter for software engineers for going on 70 years now, will be done by machine. Humans will specify, and humans will QA the results, and that's it."},"story_title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"partial","matchedWords":["ai"],"value":"<em>AI</em> Can Write More of the Code. We Still Have to Engineer the System"},"story_url":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"partial","matchedWords":["ai"],"value":"https://picon.dev/en/publications/<em>ai</em>-assisted-engineering/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_bitwize","story_50331998"],"author":"bitwize","comment_text":"...For now.<p>Frontier models are generating sensible abstract engineering decisions for nontrivial code bases. Phase 1 (GPT 4 era) was &quot;fancy autocomplete&quot;: it could generate a function or complete a clause before losing the plot. Phase 2 (Opus 4.x era) was the spec-driven development era, involving careful construction of guardrails and guidelines and steering the agents as they worked. Phase 3 is the beginning of the &quot;software factory&quot; era. You can now one-shot nontrivial apps with a prompt that leaves most of the specifics up to the agents. You just can&#x27;t do this for like, a whole enterprise\u2014maybe a microservice.<p>Another phase or two, and you can pretty much say goodbye to 95% of SWE jobs. The only people left involved in the software development process will be business analysts, product managers, and perhaps designers if they don&#x27;t want the UI to look all &quot;AI ass-ugly&quot;. A handful of trillion-dollar companies will retain a few really smart people who <i>could</i> code by hand if they wanted to, to keep the models trained and topped up on good practices. But line-of-business, which was the bread and butter for software engineers for going on 70 years now, will be done by machine. Humans will specify, and humans will QA the results, and that&#x27;s it.","created_at":"2026-11-02T02:56:17Z","created_at_i":1793588177,"objectID":"50332614","parent_id":50331998,"story_id":50331998,"story_title":"AI Can Write More of the Code. We Still Have to Engineer the System","story_url":"https://picon.dev/en/publications/ai-assisted-engineering/","updated_at":"2026-11-02T03:06:25Z"}],"hitsPerPage":3,"nbHits":306,"nbPages":102,"page":0,"params":"query=AI+agent+cost&tags=%28story%2Ccomment%29&hitsPerPage=3&page=0&numericFilters=created_at_i%3E%3D1791004054%2Ccreated_at_i%3C%3D1793596054&advancedSyntax=true&analyticsTags=backend","processingTimeMS":19,"processingTimingsMS":{"_request":{"roundTrip":23},"fetch":{"query":14,"scanning":3,"total":18},"total":19},"query":"AI agent cost","serverTimeMS":20}
